Track B also fails for the native distribution. The planted set is uniformly random and the remaining edges are independently random, so the instance contains no compact label pattern, symmetry, or change of variables that identifies the witness. At \(N=1000,d=40,\rho=0.13\), one five-sweep/19-replica PT round already entails about 3.8 million local neighbor operations; after “seeing” the PT insight, the solver still has to execute that same route. That is a large algorithmic workload but no compression gap, so it tests unavailable computation rather than mathematical insight. I’m documenting this as a Step-0 H rejection on both tracks.
